=: title status-17 - fall update
=: date 2026-10-25
=: tags status
=: draft true

pico.sh is a set of services for developers that you use over SSH. Your SSH key is your account, and the tools you already have are the client. You publish a blog with rsync, host a static site with scp, share localhost with `ssh -R`, and stream data between machines with a pipe.
=> https://pico.sh

Our last big roundup was in January. Since then we've rewritten how rsync works on the platform, rebuilt the tuns console, brought lists back to prose, and added a lot to pgs and pipe. Here's what changed.
=> /status-014 status-14 - a new year

# rsync, natively

Every service that takes files accepts uploads from the rsync you already have installed. Until this month that went through a minimal rsync receiver. It handled the common cases, but every new flag meant another special case, and delta transfers and compression were out of reach.

We replaced it with a native Go implementation of the rsync protocol, versions 27 through 31. Our server now behaves like any other rsync server:

Unchanged files are skipped by size and modification time, or by content with `-c`
Changed files are sent as a delta against our copy
`-z` compression with zlib or zlibx
`--exclude`, `--include`, merge files and the other filter rules
Every `--delete` mode, `--dry-run`, `--relative` and `--files-from`
Downloads work the same way, with deltas and compression

=< /status-17-rsync.gif uploading a site with rsync, then uploading it again after changing one line

File data streams to and from our object storage through fixed-size buffers. A 1 GB upload peaks at about 5 MB of memory on our side. We also sped up pgs uploads and serving while we were in there.
=> https://pico.sh/file-uploads supported rsync options

# tuns

tuns gives you HTTP, TCP, TLS and UDP tunnels to localhost over SSH. It runs on sish, the open source tunneling server we maintain.
=> https://pico.sh/tuns
=> https://github.com/antoniomika/sish

=< /tuns-console-requests.png the tuns service console

Every HTTP tunnel comes with a service console, and we rebuilt it from scratch. It shows requests live with formatted JSON and form bodies, inline images, "Copy as curl" and replay. Filters take quoted phrases and `-term` to exclude. WebSocket connections show every frame in both directions. Streamed responses like server-sent events are captured as they arrive, and gzip, deflate, brotli and zstd bodies are decoded.
=> /ann-039-tuns-service-console a new service console for tuns

=< /status-17-tuns.gif opening a tunnel with the tunnel script and watching requests arrive

You can also ask tuns how to open a tunnel. `ssh tuns.sh http 3000 dev` prints the ssh command for an HTTP tunnel, and there are commands for https, tcp, sni and udp. `ssh tuns.sh script` prints a POSIX shell function so you can just type `tunnel http 3000`.
=> /ann-040-tuns-commands tuns commands and the tunnel script

Smaller changes:

`host-header` now works with HTTPS upstreams, so you can put a tunnel in front of a remote HTTPS site
Custom domains can use a `_tuns` TXT record, and `_sish` still works
If your tunnel drops partway through a response, the visitor gets an error instead of a cut-off body that looks complete

# pgs

pgs is our static site host. You upload with rsync, scp or sftp and your site is live.
=> https://pico.sh/pgs

Auto-forms: point an HTML form at `/pgs/forms/{name}` and we collect the submissions, which you read back with `ssh pgs.sh forms {name}`
Password-protected sites with `ssh pgs.sh acl {project} --type http-pass --acl {password}`
Projects prefixed with `private-` are private and can't be made public
A `bin` project whose files delete themselves after two weeks, for quick sharing
Uploads straight from a unix pipe: `echo "<p>hi</p>" | ssh pgs.sh /bin/index.html`
The per-file limit for pico+ went from 50MB to 100MB
We replaced our third-party HTTP cache with our own RFC 9111 implementation
=< /status-17-http-pass.png the login page of a password-protected pgs site
=> /ann-035-pgs-features new pgs features

# prose

prose is our blog platform. You write markdown and rsync it up.
=> https://prose.sh

Our plain text lists format is back. Write a post as a `.lxt` file and it renders as a list, and this post is one.
=> /ann-034-plain-text-lists plain text lists

We also gave every blog a new minimal default design: centered content, a 70ch max width, and headers anchored to their sections. Discovery now has a trending feed, and you can rename a post in place over sftp.
=> /ann-039-prose-blog-redesign prose blog redesign

# pipe

pipe is authenticated pubsub over SSH. If you know how to use `|`, you know how to use pipe.
=> https://pipe.pico.sh

Monitors: `ssh pipe.pico.sh monitor my-cron 1h` expects a publish to `my-cron` at least once an hour and alerts you through an RSS feed when one doesn't arrive
Wildcard subscriptions: `ssh pipe.pico.sh sub 'deploys-*'`
Round-robin delivery with `pub -d round_robin`, so a topic can feed a pool of workers
`?prefix=` on pipe-web to split a stream into messages
=< /status-17-pipe.gif a wildcard subscriber receiving messages from three topics
=< /status-17-monitor.gif creating a monitor, pinging it and checking its status
=> https://pico.sh/pipe pipe docs

# the platform

Free accounts now need an invite from an existing user or a pico+ member. Bots found our free services this year and flooded prose's discovery page, and invites let us keep a free tier instead of putting everything behind pico+. If you want one and don't know anyone, ask us on IRC or email hello@pico.sh.
=> /ann-038-pico-invite-system introducing the pico invite system

`ssh pico.sh dns-check example.com` shows where a domain points and checks its TXT records for pgs, prose and tuns
`ssh pico.sh access-logs` lists every time one of your keys signed in over the last 30 days, labeled with the key's name
Site analytics now break visits down by device
`ssh pico.sh` with no command, or one it doesn't know, prints help instead of hanging

=< /status-17-dnscheck.gif checking a custom domain's records with dns-check

# what's next

We're building a CI service and a CD service that run entirely over SSH. They run on our own hardware in a colo, and the beta sites are up.
=> https://ci.pico.sh pici
=> https://cd.pico.sh picd

We're also working on patchbin, our git collaboration service where `ssh pr.pico.sh pr create` replaces `git send-email`.
=> https://pr.pico.sh patchbin

That's it. Come say hi on IRC.
=> https://pico.sh/irc
